{
  "version": 208,
  "released": "2026-08-24",
  "changelog": "v208: the placement score — the certainty line's arithmetic. WHY: the owner asked for a level (\"how placed is this life, 0 → 1\") and already had the margin (the star's \"one answer would place 53 things\"). Issue #637's finding is that they are ONE arithmetic — the interval-width sum timeline.unknown_width has ranked the Reading Room's plan on since v204, because a threshold count is not submodular and a width-sum is — and building them apart would repeat the promise/delivery drift the dating-dataflow audit already found once, where dependency_index counted what an anchor TOUCHED and no pass could deliver any of it. Research authority: system/research/chronology-vis.md (v206). FIX (1) UNKNOWN_YEARS — one definition of the interval a thing occupies ABSENT AN ANSWER, with three consumers: the score's per-thing width, the certainty chart's cloud dot and tap-span, and the ghost's prior span. A moment takes its band's span (containment read as bounds), or the life where its era has none; a period_bound takes its derived span, else the hole between its dated neighbours on the spine, else the life; a place_span takes its band's; era_gap, residence_gap and date_contradiction keep the interval they already mint (the last of which is now computed in timeline_corroboration where the two disputed claims are, rather than parsed back out of the rendered sentence). life = (birth_year, current_year) from the birth landmark, and with no birthday there is no floor and none is invented. unknown_width now finds a real interval where it used to hit its 1.0 ranking floor; the floor STAYS as the no-interval fallback so go-deep.md §8.2's degeneration property still holds on a birthless vault. (2) THE SCORE — timeline.placement_score(data) computes 1 − Σwᵢ/(n·L) over every thing the timeline holds (placed moments, unplaced moments, eras, place spans; ONE enumeration shared with the tests). Four rules travel with it and are not optional. WIDTH, NEVER PRESENCE: a field-filled meter is an improper scoring rule in Gneiting & Raftery's exact sense, maximised by writing anything down, while the ladder stores a hedge as a WIDER interval with a weaker confidence — guessing cannot pay, and the score is downstream of the never-propose-a-date lint and does not police itself. A FLOOR: marginal width sums overestimate disorder wherever ordering constraints exist, by 3× in Mountakis, Klos & Witteveen's worked case, so caveat_floor is True and stays True until the concurrent-flexibility correction lands (the one named follow-up in system/research/QUEUE.md). A PAIR: score_stated recomputes with every derived record read as undated, so the cross-dating pass moves score and CANNOT move score_stated — pinned by test, and it needed two things beyond the obvious one (on the stated basis an UNDATED thing is read against the stated view too, or it inherits the era span the pass just derived; and a band place's envelope span now carries the date_derived marker ADR 0026 says is the only marker, or the pair reads an envelope the pass produced as something the person stated). AND NOT A VERDICT: band is 1..5 on fixed, arbitrary but STABLE thresholds, never peer-relative (there are no peers in a private vault), and the word is placement — never \"completeness\", never \"accuracy\". No birth landmark means no score at all and the block is simply absent, which is why birth wears the ★. (3) THE STRIP — per_year_band gives one row per calendar year, each thing contributing min(1, 1/wᵢ) to every year it covers, normalised by how many things cover it: Ratcliffe & McCullagh's aoristic weight with a uniform prior. That is the half that answers Crema's summation problem — five moments smeared over five blocks and five pinned inside them sum IDENTICALLY, and the strip tells them apart — and the Crema separation golden is the load-bearing test of the whole change. An empty year reads 0, honestly: a flat stretch means nobody asked. (4) THE MARGIN — next_gain is the top row of the EXISTING greedy plan re-expressed in score units: the level recomputed with that anchor's resolve set collapsed to the anchor's own grain (ANCHOR_GRAIN_YEARS = one year, the coarsest thing the ladder accepts as placed, so a finer answer over-delivers and the margin stays a floor too), minus the level now. It re-runs the same _level over a copied population — cross_dating.gain_sentence_for_record's promise-equals-delivery discipline applied to a number instead of a sentence — and the golden files the answer and checks the realized delta matches. keystones() is untouched. (5) THE GHOST — every DATED moment gains prior_span, what its interval would be absent its date, stamped on the pass's own walk so stated and derived moments alike carry it, and absent where the reconstruction is not wider than the moment's own interval. Reconstructed, never stored: after an era's dates improve, old ghosts TIGHTEN on the next read, which is the stateless trade stated plainly rather than fixed by keeping history. (6) THE GLOW — unknown_anchor(row) maps a row to the anchor it would BECOME, and every offered row gains resolves (that anchor's set minus its own key) and leverage (1 + len(resolves), self-inclusive, so a row with no reach is exactly 1). This REDEFINES row leverage, which since v196 was the size of the largest resolve set a row happened to belong to — a row ranked by what somebody else's answer would do for it. An unplaced moment and a residence gap honestly name no anchor. The glow is RELATIVE and its rank-quantiles are the host's job. NO VISUAL SHIPS HERE: the score is a number in the payload, rendered nowhere until the platform half. PLATFORM: every addition is additive — placement, counts.placement_band, unknowns[].years, unknowns[].leverage/.resolves, event_lineup[…][].prior_span; TimelineData gains placement (default {}) and every other addition rides existing JsonObject rows. TESTS: tests/test_placement_score.py carries the Crema separation, monotonicity, the immovable pair, promise≈delivery, no-birth absence, the per-kind unknown_years table, the ghost (including the founder's \"Born in Redlands\" shape), and the heavy-tail glow.",
